<section class="section showcase arch" id="ai-tools">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">How the AI knows the data</p>
        <h2>It looks things up.<br>It never guesses.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>The model doesn't come with your account in its head. Every question is sent with instructions and a list
          of <b>tools</b>; the model replies "call this tool", spendlens runs it and returns the result, and the model
          decides the next step. Without <code>--live</code> it has three tools over your saved runs. With
          <code>--live</code> it gets five more that cover the whole Steampipe AWS plugin.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>8 tools</li><li>All read-only</li><li>Schema from the plugin's source</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("ai_tools") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="ai-knowledge">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">Where its knowledge comes from</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Four sources. No guessing.</h2>
    <p class="sub">The model sees none of these all at once. It reads only the part it needs, one tool call at a
      time, so a question costs a few thousand tokens, not the whole AWS plugin.</p>
    <div class="bento tools-bento">
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["clock"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">1 · History schema</p>
        <h3>Your saved runs.</h3>
        <p>The <code>history_query</code> tool description includes the history database's tables and columns.
          <code>history_overview</code> adds which runs exist, their periods and dimensions, and the exact service
          names. <code>history_cost</code> returns one service's whole cost story in one call, for
          "why did RDS go up?".</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["book"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">2 · AWS table catalog</p>
        <h3>Built from the plugin itself.</h3>
        <p>At release time spendlens reads the Steampipe AWS plugin's Go source and docs (v1.35.0, the version it
          installs) and ships <code>aws_catalog.json.gz</code>: for each of 589 tables its columns with meanings,
          required and fast filters, up to 3 examples, and cost or time-range notes.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["list"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">3 · Your installed plugin</p>
        <h3>The real column list.</h3>
        <p>With <code>--live</code>, spendlens reads <code>information_schema.columns</code> from your local Steampipe
          once a day. That describes the installed plugin, not AWS, so it's free. If your plugin version differs, its
          columns win and the catalog adds descriptions and filter rules.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["spark"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">4 · The model + the checker</p>
        <h3>General SQL, checked.</h3>
        <p>The model brings general SQL and Postgres knowledge. Anything it writes goes through the checker first: a
          wrong column comes back as "did you mean…", a missing required filter with the exact fix, before anything
          reaches AWS.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="arch-table-wrap"><table class="arch-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>When</th><th>What the model gets back</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>history_overview</code></td><td>always</td><td>The latest run per feature (time, account, totals),
          each cost run's periods and dimensions, service names by spend, the history schema.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>history_cost</code></td><td>always</td><td>One service's saved cost in every cost run, any
          breakdown inside it, and its rightsizing, waste, savings and tag findings. Short names work:
          <code>rds</code>, <code>ec2</code>, <code>nat</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>history_query</code></td><td>always</td><td>Rows from one read-only SQLite <code>SELECT</code> over
          runs, findings and cost rows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aws_find_tables</code></td><td><code>--live</code></td><td>The best ~8 of 589 tables for the
          question, by name, service, description and column, with synonyms (volume → EBS, ACU → RDS cluster).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aws_describe_table</code></td><td><code>--live</code></td><td>One table's columns and their
          meaning, required filters with valid values, fast filters, examples, cost notes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aws_check_query</code></td><td><code>--live</code></td><td>Problems in a <code>SELECT</code> and how
          to fix them, without calling AWS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aws_query</code></td><td><code>--live</code></td><td>Rows with an "as of" time: checked, then from
          the cache if fresh, else run as a read-only job.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>aws_query_result</code></td><td><code>--live</code></td><td>The rows of a slow query that was still
          running, after waiting again.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="arch-note">Look up exactly what the model sees: <code>spendlens schema find "aurora capacity"</code>,
      <code>spendlens schema show aws_rds_db_cluster</code>. One limit: the catalog knows a column holds JSON, but not
      every key inside it, so for those the model relies on descriptions and examples.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch" id="ask-live">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Sequence · AI + Steampipe AWS plugin</p>
        <h2>Any AWS question,<br>read-only.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>With <code>--live</code> the assistant can answer from any of the AWS plugin's 589 tables, not only cost:
          inventory, security groups, IAM, Lambda runtimes and more. It looks up the real tables and columns first,
          every query is checked before it reaches AWS, slow queries run as jobs it waits for, and recent results
          are reused from the cache.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>No guessed columns</li><li>Waits for slow queries</li><li>Cached by freshness</li><li>Your IAM role decides what it can see</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("seq_ask_live") }}</div>
