<section class="section" id="schema-cache">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">Schema &amp; cache commands</p>
    <h2 class="h2">The assistant's tools.<br>Yours too.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Each layer of the pipeline is also a command. The schema commands run offline from the bundled
      catalog: no AWS calls, no Steampipe and no AI provider. They see exactly what the model sees.</p>
    <div class="bento tools-bento">
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["book"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">1 · Catalog</p>
        <h3>Find the table. Read its rules.</h3>
        <p><code>schema find</code> runs the search behind <code>aws_find_tables</code>; <code>schema show</code>
          prints what <code>aws_describe_table</code> returns: columns and their meaning, required filters with valid
          values, fast filters, cost and time notes, examples.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens schema find "unattached EBS volumes"
spendlens schema show aws_cost_usage --no-examples
spendlens schema show aws_ebs_volume --json</code></pre>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["list"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">2 · Checker</p>
        <h3>Check SQL before AWS sees it.</h3>
        <p><code>schema check</code> is <code>aws_check_query</code>: the same check that runs before every live
          query. Exit code 1 when the query would fail, so it works as a CI gate for your own Steampipe SQL.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens schema check "select size_gb from aws_ebs_volume"
# ✗ column size_gb does not exist in aws_ebs_volume
#   did you mean: size</code></pre>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["clock"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">3 · Runner</p>
        <h3>Waits, within limits you set.</h3>
        <p>Live queries run only through <code>ask --live</code>, as background jobs on read-only connections.
          <code>llm.query_wait_s</code> sets how long each call waits before the model waits again;
          <code>llm.query_timeout_s</code> cancels the query.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens ask --live --show-tools "Idle NAT gateways?"
spendlens ask --live --set llm.query_timeout_s=600 "…"</code></pre>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["coins"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">4 · Cache</p>
        <h3>See what's reused. Clear it.</h3>
        <p><code>ai-cache.db</code> sits next to the history: live results by data class, answers by data
          fingerprint, brief summaries by their figures. Scoped to your connection, regions and AWS identity.
          <code>--fresh</code> skips it once.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens cache show --entries
spendlens cache clear --queries
spendlens cache clear --answers
spendlens brief --fresh -o brief.html</code></pre>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch alt" id="sessions">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Sequence · Sessions</p>
        <h2>Follow-ups that<br>remember.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>A plain <code>spendlens ask</code> forgets everything when it ends. Start a <b>session</b> and each question
          and answer is saved to a small JSON file next to the history. The next <code>ask -C</code> replays the
          last 12 exchanges to the model, so "those" and "the second one" make sense, even in a new terminal on
          another day.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>Any provider</li><li>Named or automatic</li><li>Save as a page</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("seq_session") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="session-how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">Sessions in practice</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Start, continue, switch, save.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Three flags on <code>ask</code> and one <code>session</code> command cover everything.</p>
    <div class="steps two">
      <div class="step"><h3>Start a new one</h3>
        <p><code>--new</code> starts a fresh session with an automatic name like <code>s-20261120-101500</code>. Add
          <code>-S NAME</code> to name it; <code>--new -S NAME</code> starts that name over.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens ask --new "Oversized ECS services?"
spendlens ask --new -S ecs "Oversized ECS services?"</code></pre></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Continue it</h3>
        <p><code>-C</code> continues the last session you used. <code>-S NAME</code> continues that session, or creates
          it if it doesn't exist yet.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens ask -C "List them by cluster"
spendlens ask -S ecs "Which have no autoscaling?"</code></pre></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Keep topics apart</h3>
        <p>Each name is its own conversation. Switching to <code>-S rds</code> doesn't mix RDS into the ECS thread, and
          <code>-C</code> then follows the one you used last.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens ask -S rds "Why did RDS go up?"
spendlens session list</code></pre></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Review, save, tidy</h3>
        <p>Show a session in the terminal, save the whole conversation as one page or Markdown, or delete it. These
          commands work offline.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens session show ecs
spendlens session show ecs -o ecs.html
spendlens session delete ecs
spendlens session clear --yes</code></pre></div>
    </div>
    <p class="arch-note">What is replayed: the last 12 questions and final answers (each answer up to 4,000 characters),
      not the earlier tool results, so follow-ups stay cheap. A follow-up is never served from the answer cache, because
      its meaning depends on the conversation. <code>-i</code> is different: it keeps a conversation in memory for one
      terminal and saves nothing unless you also pass <code>--new</code> or <code>-S</code>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="ask-flags">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">spendlens ask flags</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Every way to ask.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Combine them freely, e.g. <code>ask --live -S ecs --show-tools "…" -o ecs.html</code>.</p>
    <div class="arch-table-wrap"><table class="arch-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><i>(none)</i></td><td>Answer from your latest saved runs only. No AWS calls.</td>
          <td><code>spendlens ask "Top 5 savings?"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--live</code></td><td>Also allow read-only live AWS queries through Steampipe: inventory and
          configuration, not only what was saved.</td><td><code>ask --live "Unattached EBS volumes?"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--source demo</code></td><td>Answer from the bundled sample history.</td>
          <td><code>ask --source demo "What changed?"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--new</code></td><td>Start a new session (with <code>-S NAME</code>: start that one over).</td>
          <td><code>ask --new "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-S, --session</code></td><td>Continue or create a named session.</td>
          <td><code>ask -S ecs "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-C, --continue</code></td><td>Continue the last session used.</td><td><code>ask -C "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-i, --interactive</code></td><td>Keep asking in one terminal (type <code>exit</code> to stop).</td>
          <td><code>ask -i</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--show-tools</code></td><td>Print each tool call as it runs: the tool, its SQL, row count, time and
          first rows.</td><td><code>ask --show-tools "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-o, --out</code></td><td>Also save the answer as a styled page (<code>.html</code>, with the tool
          calls) or <code>.md</code>.</td><td><code>ask "…" -o answer.html</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>Machine-readable answer, token usage and full audit trail.</td>
          <td><code>ask --json "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--max-steps</code></td><td>Tool-call limit for this question (default <code>llm.max_steps</code>,
          12). At the limit the model still answers from what it found, marked as possibly incomplete.</td>
          <td><code>ask --max-steps 20 "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--fresh</code></td><td>Skip cached answers and live results this time (new results are still
          cached).</td><td><code>ask --live --fresh "…"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-c</code>, <code>-p</code>, <code>--set</code></td><td>Same as every command: policy file, AWS
          profile, and one-off settings such as the model.</td><td><code>--set llm.model=claude-sonnet-5-5</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="agent-loop">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">How the AI works</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Plan, query, check, answer.</h2>
    <p class="sub">The model is a planner, not an operator. It can only ask spendlens to run one of its tools, and every
      tool reads.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><h3>You ask</h3>
        <p>spendlens sends your question, the FinOps analyst instructions and the tool list to your AI provider:
          Bedrock, Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI or any OpenAI-compatible server (Ollama, vLLM, GLM).</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>It learns the schema</h3>
        <p>It starts with your saved runs. With <code>--live</code> it searches the AWS table catalog
          (<code>aws_find_tables</code>) and reads the chosen table's columns, required filters and examples
          (<code>aws_describe_table</code>), so it never guesses a column.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Its query is checked</h3>
        <p>Before anything reaches AWS, spendlens checks the <code>SELECT</code>: read-only, real tables and columns,
          required filters present, JSON operators on JSON columns, a date range on Cost Explorer. A mistake comes
          back with the exact fix.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Steampipe calls AWS, patiently</h3>
        <p>A fresh cached result is used if there is one. Otherwise the query runs as a background job: the AWS
          plugin pages through <code>Describe*</code> / <code>List*</code> calls, and if that takes a while the model
          waits for the job (<code>aws_query_result</code>) instead of failing.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>It repeats, then remembers</h3>
        <p>Rows go back as data, never as instructions; it may refine or join, up to <code>llm.max_steps</code>.
          At the limit it is asked for its best answer from what it found. The answer is cached: the same question on the same data costs no tokens until a new run is saved or the
          live data's freshness window ends.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>You check</h3>
        <p>The answer cites the SQL and numbers it used. <code>--show-tools</code> prints every call, and
          <code>--json</code> saves the full audit trail. Savings are proposals for you to review.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase ai" id="safety">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">Safety</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Three read-only layers.<br>Zero writes.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Every query the AI assistant writes must pass the parser, then runs on a connection that cannot
      write: the read-only history, or with <code>--live</code>, a read-only Steampipe session.</p>
    <div class="layers">
      <article class="layer"><b>1</b><h3>SQL guard</h3>
        <p>The query is parsed with sqlglot. Only one read-only <code>SELECT</code> passes: no writes, DDL,
          <code>SELECT INTO</code>, multiple statements, <code>SET</code>, or functions such as
          <code>pg_read_file</code>.</p></article>
      <article class="layer"><b>2</b><h3>Read-only history</h3>
        <p>The SQLite history opens in read-only mode with <code>query_only</code> and an authorizer that denies
          everything except reads.</p></article>
      <article class="layer"><b>3</b><h3>Read-only sessions</h3>
        <p>Live Steampipe queries run in sessions with <code>default_transaction_read_only</code>, a statement
          timeout and a row cap.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="grid arch-more">
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["key"]|safe }}</span><h3>Credentials</h3>
        <p>The AWS plugin uses the standard credential chain: the role assumed in your terminal,
          <code>AWS_PROFILE</code>, SSO, or OIDC in CI. spendlens stores no AWS keys.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["spark"]|safe }}</span><h3>Any LLM, native SDKs</h3>
        <p>Claude through the official Anthropic SDK (Anthropic API or Bedrock with your AWS role); OpenAI, Azure OpenAI
          and compatible servers through the official OpenAI SDK.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["flask"]|safe }}</span><h3>Tested at three layers</h3>
        <p>Unit tests; integration tests with the real SDKs and Powerpipe; system tests that run the CLI end to end
          through Steampipe against an AWS emulator.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

